WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.440
A muscle fibre shortens, yet none of its filaments gets any shorter.

2
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:12.980
How can that be? Let's zoom in and watch one contract.

3
00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:16.923
A skeletal muscle fibre is one long cell.

4
00:00:18.183 --> 00:00:21.523
It is packed with chains of repeating units called sarcomeres.

5
00:00:22.843 --> 00:00:25.503
Each sarcomere runs from one Z disc to the next.

6
00:00:26.567 --> 00:00:29.607
Thin filaments of actin hang from each Z disc.

7
00:00:30.787 --> 00:00:33.247
A thick filament of myosin sits in the middle.

8
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:36.927
Its heads stick out toward the actin on both sides.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.900
At rest, a strand called tropomyosin covers the sites where myosin binds.

10
00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:45.480
Calcium is the switch.

11
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:51.840
In skeletal and heart muscle, it binds troponin, and troponin moves tropomyosin

12
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:52.840
aside.

13
00:00:53.333 --> 00:00:55.993
Now the myosin heads grab actin and swing.

14
00:00:57.493 --> 00:00:59.113
Heads on each half pull toward the middle.

15
00:01:00.373 --> 00:01:02.753
So both sets of thin filaments slide inward.

16
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:07.173
The Z discs draw together, and the sarcomere shortens.

17
00:01:08.313 --> 00:01:11.793
But the thick filament keeps its length, and so do the thin ones.

18
00:01:13.033 --> 00:01:14.413
Only their overlap changes.

19
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:17.777
Each head pulls only a tiny distance.

20
00:01:19.117 --> 00:01:21.697
Then it must let go and grab again further along.

21
00:01:23.137 --> 00:01:25.297
ATP binds, and the head lets go.

22
00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:28.877
ATP is split, and the head re-cocks.

23
00:01:30.177 --> 00:01:32.817
Then it grabs actin again and swings once more.

24
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.760
Here is a question for you.

25
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:37.940
Take away all the ATP.

26
00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.220
Do the heads stay stuck to actin, or do they fall off?

27
00:01:48.717 --> 00:01:55.257
They stay stuck. Without ATP, an attached head cannot let go, so the fibre locks

28
00:01:55.257 --> 00:02:00.917
stiff. That is why muscles stiffen after death, the state called rigor mortis.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.060
One sarcomere moves only a hair.

30
00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.420
So how does that become a pull you can see?

31
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.820
The answer is in how sarcomeres are arranged.

32
00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:15.677
A long fibre holds tens of thousands of sarcomeres in a row.

33
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:18.237
Each one shortens a little.

34
00:02:19.537 --> 00:02:23.657
Their small shortenings add up along the chain into one visible pull.

35
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:26.740
Notice what this machine can do.

36
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:29.320
It can pull its ends together.

37
00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:31.860
It cannot push them apart.

38
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:35.613
So every muscle needs something to stretch it back.

39
00:02:36.893 --> 00:02:40.873
In your arm, one muscle bends the elbow and another straightens it.

40
00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:43.133
Each takes a turn pulling.

41
00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:46.427
Filaments slide; they never shrink.

42
00:02:47.787 --> 00:02:50.427
Many small slides in a row add up to one pull.

43
00:02:51.747 --> 00:02:54.467
Every muscle in this lesson pulls with this same machine.

44
00:02:55.807 --> 00:02:57.947
They differ in how calcium throws the switch.
